 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "conscio-lumine",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Personification",
   "quote": "conscio lumine",
   "trans": "with the lamp my accomplice",
   "body": "Psyche calls the lamp her <em>conscius</em> — \"accomplice, fellow-conspirator\". The light that helped her break the taboo is cast as a partner in crime, a wry, knowing touch in her retelling (and an echo of the lamp that betrayed her).",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The personification of the <em>conscium lumen</em> (‘the lamp, my accomplice’) wittily makes the light a partner in Psyche’s transgression, recalling the very lamp whose spilled oil ruined everything.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "ipsum-ipsum",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Emphatic Repetition",
   "quote": "ipsum illum ... filium, ipsum inquam Cupidinem",
   "trans": "that very son ... Cupid himself, I mean",
   "body": "The doubled <em>ipsum ... ipsum</em>, with the breathless aside <em>inquam</em> (\"I mean\"), conveys Psyche’s astonishment as she relives the revelation: not a monster, but the son of Venus — Cupid himself.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The repeated <em>ipsum ... ipsum ... inquam</em> dramatises Psyche’s wonder as she recounts the discovery, the stammering emphasis re-enacting the shock of seeing Cupid rather than the promised beast.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "fruendi-inopia",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Paradox",
   "quote": "fruendi laborarem inopia",
   "trans": "I was struggling with the inability to enjoy it",
   "body": "A neat paradox of desire: overwhelmed by a <em>copia</em> (\"abundance\") of longing, Psyche suffers an <em>inopia</em> (\"lack/helplessness\") — she cannot enjoy what she has. The antithesis of plenty and want captures the agony of love.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The paradox <em>copia ... inopia</em> (abundance versus lack) and the phrase <em>fruendi inopia</em> express the torment of overwhelming desire that cannot be satisfied, a typically Apuleian play of opposites.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "sororem-coniugabo",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Dramatic Irony",
   "quote": "sororem tuam ... mihi confarreatis nuptiis coniugabo",
   "trans": "I will join your sister to me in official marriage",
   "body": "Here is Psyche’s revenge in motion: she pretends Cupid means to take the <em>sister</em> as his solemn wife (<em>confarreatae nuptiae</em> = the most binding Roman marriage). The lie, named to flatter, will lure the jealous sister to her death — a trap baited with her own greed.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The invented promise that Cupid will marry the sister in <em>confarreatae nuptiae</em> is Psyche’s cunning revenge: the dramatic irony is that the listener, flattered and greedy, will rush to the cliff and her doom.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "zephyro-efflaret",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Closure",
   "quote": "Zephyro praecipit ... me ... efflaret",
   "trans": "he ordered the West Wind to blow me away",
   "body": "Psyche’s tale ends as her happiness began — with the <em>Zephyrus</em>. Once the West Wind carried her gently to Cupid’s palace; now the same wind is commanded to <em>efflare</em> (\"blow\") her out of it. The symmetry seals her expulsion.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The return of <em>Zephyrus</em> frames Psyche’s story: the wind that first bore her to the palace now blows her out of it, the ring-composition underlining how completely her fortune has reversed.\"",
